;; flint's own standard library, as a WORKSPACE (`DECISIONS.md#workspace-capabilities`).
;;
;; It is here for the NAME. Without it a workspace's identity falls back to the
;; source root's path, which is machine-specific and reads badly in a
;; diagnostic -- "guarded by /Users/someone/checkouts/flint/lib" says nothing a
;; reader can act on -- and it would differ from the name the SDK uses for the
;; same files, which is `flint/flint`. Two front doors naming one workspace two
;; ways is the defect this whole mechanism exists to stop.
;;
;; AND IT HOLDS `:host`, which it did not used to. This file said "It grants
;; NOTHING ... the standard library holds no capability of its own", and that
;; was true while the guard was only on the VAR: `flint.host/request` was
;; guarded, and its body reached the host through a builtin that nothing
;; checked. Guarding the builtin made the sentence false. `flint.host/request`
;; and `flint.host/ask` call `flint.rt/request`, so the library that implements
;; the capability must hold it.
;;
;; A GRANT AND NOT AN EXEMPTION, and the difference is the whole point. The
;; first attempt let a var's own guard authorise its own body -- which reads
;; well, since that is exactly the wrapper relationship -- and it was a way to
;; MINT authority: a guard is written by the author of the var, about their own
;; var, so any workspace could assert one, call the builtin from behind it, and
;; re-export it through an unguarded function. Measured, and it compiled. A
;; grant is different in kind: it is conferred from outside, by the embedder's
;; workspace table or by this file, which ships with the compiler.
;;
;; It is still the narrowest true statement. What it does NOT do is let a
;; caller past `flint.host/request`'s own guard -- an application still holds
;; `:host` or it does not.
{:flint/workspace flint/flint
 :flint/capabilities-grant [:host]}
